course: dictionary
lesson: 12199
title: A huge fire, or a huge fight
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A conflagration is a very large fire that is destructive, which means it breaks and destroys things, and it spreads over a wide area. Writers use it when a fire is much bigger than an ordinary blaze. «The conflagration spread from the bakery across the whole street in one hour.» The word tells you the fire was huge and out of control.
explain: The word has a second meaning, used more in news and old writing: a big war or conflict that spreads. «The news warned that a conflagration could spread across the whole region.» Here nothing is burning. The clue is that the word spreads, like a fire, and the sentence is about nations or armies. Context helps you choose the right meaning.
explain: Conflagration is a formal word, so you will meet it more in history books, newspapers and older stories than in everyday talk. When you read it, try swapping in the simple words «a huge fire» or «a blaze», or «a big war», and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Conflagration is a noun, so you can say a conflagration or conflagrations.
fact: «Conflagration» means a very large, destructive fire that spreads widely.
fact: In its second meaning, «conflagration» means a big war or conflict.
fact: The Great Fire of London in 1666 and the Great Chicago Fire in 1871 are famous examples of conflagrations.
fact: Everyday English says «a huge fire» or «a blaze», so these are good simple swaps.
exercise: «The conflagration spread across the whole forest in a single night.» What is a conflagration? | *A very large, destructive fire | A small candle flame | A kind of weather map | A quiet, cool breeze
exercise: In everyday English, which word is closest in meaning to conflagration? | *blaze | puddle | breeze | whisper
exercise: Mia reads in a history book: «The conflagration of 1666 destroyed much of London.» Which event is this? | A quiet picnic in the park | *A huge fire in London in 1666 | A new bridge built over the river | A school sports day
test: «The old newspaper described a conflagration between the two nations, a war that spread fast.» What does conflagration mean here? | A fire in a kitchen | A kind of festival | A cold, quiet winter | *A big war or conflict
test: Hannah reads, «A conflagration swept through the town.» Which swap keeps the meaning? | Gentle rain swept through the town | *Huge fire swept through the town | Quiet music swept through the town | Tiny puppy swept through the town
exam: «The conflagration of the war spread across the whole region.» What does conflagration describe in this sentence? | A kind of flower | *A huge war or conflict that spreads | A gentle sleep | A small picnic
